   "text": "I read the drawing region by region and assembled the SMILES from the pieces below.\n\n**Macrocycle, going around the ring**\n- **Ornithine residue:** H2N\u2013CH2CH2CH2 side chain (three CH2 groups) on the alpha carbon, which carries a wedge bond to the chain. Its ring NH is bonded to a CH2 on the pyridine, and its C=O is bonded to the lysine NH.\n- **Lysine residue:** a four-CH2 side chain ending in NH2, and an alpha carbon drawn with a wedge. Its C=O is bonded to the N-methyl nitrogen of the next residue.\n- **N-methyl tryptophan residue:** N(CH3), then an alpha carbon with a wedged CH2 to indol-3-yl (the indole NH is drawn). Its C=O is bonded to an NH, which is bonded to a CH2 on the central benzene ring.\n- **Central benzene ring:**\n  - one carbon carries the CH2\u2013NH;\n  - the adjacent carbon carries the S bridge;\n  - the next carbon carries Cl;\n  - the fourth ring carbon is CH;\n  - the fifth ring carbon is CH;\n  - the sixth carbon, adjacent to the CH2-bearing carbon, carries the pyridyl group.\n- **S-linked pyridine:** a 2-pyridyl whose C3 carries the CH2\u2013NH of the ornithine.\n- **Aryl substituent:** a pyridine bonded through its C5 and carrying COOH at C2 (a picolinic acid).\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nThe CORN rule applied to the wedges shows all three alpha carbons have the L configuration. This gives @@ for the ornithine carbon as written in the SMILES, and @ for the lysine and tryptophan carbons.\n\nSMILES: NCCC[C@@H]1NCc2cccnc2Sc2c(CNC(=O)[C@H](Cc3c[nH]c4ccccc34)N(C)C(=O)[C@H](CCCCN)NC1=O)c(-c1ccc(C(=O)O)nc1)ccc2Cl"
